    {
      "parameters": {
        "width": 1760,
        "height": 380,
        "content": "## WhatsApp entry\nThe trigger's \"Receive Message Status Updates\" option receives none, so a status callback (sent/delivered/read/failed) starts no execution. Meta can still batch several changes into one call, so \"Process message\" runs in mode each: one process-message run per change, not one run reading only the first. \"Is there a message\" stops a reaction, a system message (e.g. a changed number), or an item with no messages array: no reply, nothing recorded. A real message is normalized into process-message's contract: channel 'whatsapp', the Unix-seconds timestamp as an ISO string via Luxon, name null when WhatsApp sent no contacts block, and text '' with type 'other' for anything but plain text. process-message does everything else. A null reply (a duplicate or a rate-limited silence) sends nothing and leaves replied_at NULL; only a send that succeeds reaches \"Mark replied\", so replied_at means Meta accepted the send, not that the patient's device got it. \"Send WhatsApp reply\" uses 3 tries, 2000 ms apart, a fixed exception to the usual 1000 ms wait.\nNOTE: when process-message throws, the execution fails into the error workflow, which emails the team, and the patient gets no reply. Ceiling: no reply during a Postgres or n8n outage. Upgrade: an error output that alerts and sends technical_issue_retry.\nNOTE: only messages[0] is processed. Ceiling: a batched webhook's extra messages are skipped. Upgrade: Split Out on messages."
      },
      "id": "a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
